#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176612} :activity/id #uuid "f441e707-bca4-5383-a476-aeddd2c81a22" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048144 :topic/id #uuid "82f08561-a294-5382-8640-2c8beeb6ee2e" :topic/math-academy-id 3555} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176612 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177094 :tutorial/content "Consider the rational equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{12}{x+3} = \\dfrac{2}{x-2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that when the equation consists of a single rational expression on each side, like the one above, then the easiest way to solve it is usually to use cross-multiplication.\n\nHowever, before we start, note that $x=-3$ and $x=2$ cannot be solutions. This is because when $x=-3,$ we get a zero in the denominator on the left-hand side, and when $x=2,$ we get a zero in the denominator on the right-hand side.\n\nWith this in mind, let's solve the equation. First, we cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12}{x+3} & =\\frac{2}{x-2} \\\\ 12\\cdot (x-2) & =(x+3)\\cdot 2 \\\\ 12x-24 & =2x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-24 & =2x+6 \\\\ 12x-24-2x & =2x+6-2x \\\\ 10x-24 & =6 \\\\ 10x-24+24 & =6+24 \\\\ 10x & =30 \\\\ \\frac{10x}{10} & =\\frac{30}{10} \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=3$ is different from $x=-3$ and $x=2,$ we accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $x=3.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "32ce7197-6c97-5ece-b45d-186993605c4f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8781 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ecfccb89-86a2-557a-b9d3-c0ad225826b2" :step/math-academy-id 143532 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176613}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176613 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175906 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175733 :question/id #uuid "1be25af9-d59f-5270-b9e1-d0c5dcd731e8" :question/math-academy-id "e-14143"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e09c5b9-5f31-52a5-b696-b2df1879d289" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048891}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176177} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176535} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498538} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498554}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing a Binomial in One Denominator"} :step/id #uuid "36c668d7-ed4b-5afb-8e0b-ee04536f38df" :step/math-academy-id 143533 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176614}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176614 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175907 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175739 :question/id #uuid "2d5b690c-bee9-5503-8f21-285b8c42d5fa" :question/math-academy-id "e-14705"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f3e1fc3-c46b-5958-92b2-0f2dc11ff961" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176421} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176474} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498588}] :knowledge-point/title "Equations Containing Negative Signs"} :step/id #uuid "5d7e1964-4293-5a03-90c4-211bf2cd63f1" :step/math-academy-id 143537 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176615}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176615 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175908 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175734 :question/id #uuid "72cf8733-9727-5a9f-8028-1dee62e5715d" :question/math-academy-id "e-14146"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "208596d0-1a45-57b2-afa5-d17e99218e38" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498563} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498568} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498597} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559812} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559882} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559887}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing Binomials in Both Denominators"} :step/id #uuid "fe911541-f3cd-5e52-abb4-31aa92a738cf" :step/math-academy-id 143541}] :activity/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing Binomials Using Cross-Multiplication" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176106}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559930} :activity/id #uuid "1b14901e-c955-5627-a3c0-254c47f97349" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048144 :topic/id #uuid "82f08561-a294-5382-8640-2c8beeb6ee2e" :topic/math-academy-id 3555} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559898 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559882 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "029cf53c-ae6d-5d8b-9043-5e9d44910d40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559023} {:answer/id #uuid "1e358410-36f2-52bb-a6b8-0503c41957c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559090} {:answer/id #uuid "2580ada9-6aad-537f-845b-f3e63bd554dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559109} {:answer/id #uuid "672174dc-c76d-54f0-9e26-076639ae8482" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559273} {:answer/id #uuid "c1fb6cc6-e2e8-5330-99ad-14aa63b61123" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559493}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559273} :answer-field/id #uuid "a808e838-88e8-51e8-8659-395345d61b0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559735}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f03fefa6-3a2e-5378-840e-6b0b6beb7bc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-102818" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{x-1}=\\frac{2}{x+3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that $x=-3$ and $x=1$ cannot be solutions since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nTo solve the equation, we first apply cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x-1} & =\\frac{2}{x+3} \\\\ 1\\cdot (x+3) & =(x-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\ x+3 & =2x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3 & =2x-2 \\\\ x+3-x & =2x-2-x \\\\ 3 & =x-2 \\\\ 3+2 & =x-2+2 \\\\ 5 & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=5$ is different from $x=-3$ and $x=1,$ we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $x=5.$"} :step/id #uuid "06eb6fa1-dfb2-5b2c-acc7-dce9cf39f5b3"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559930 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559887 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "174a201d-f58e-5ec3-aa26-3df65a0919fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559069} {:answer/id #uuid "1b9ee885-f816-536a-9a4b-66917e71b76e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559080} {:answer/id #uuid "3fd1f6a6-e59a-5fdb-8f79-06248ac10397" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559180} {:answer/id #uuid "6c45b276-24f3-5a57-bccd-2046b40c3894" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{3}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559285} {:answer/id #uuid "a2f762a9-8f3f-5725-8c3d-a647af718df0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559426}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559069} :answer-field/id #uuid "f814dc0c-188f-574f-b854-176c72a87e74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f7ee9ca4-a3f0-5a8a-a26b-4c46c8822ae6" :question/math-academy-id "q-47794" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{9}{6x-3}=\\frac{2}{3x+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ cannot be solutions since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nTo solve the equation, we first apply cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{6x-3} & =\\frac{2}{3x+1} \\\\ 9\\cdot (3x+1) & =(6x-3)\\cdot 2 \\\\ 27x+9 & =12x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}27x+9 & =12x-6 \\\\ 27x+9-12x & =12x-6-12x \\\\ 15x+9 & =-6 \\\\ 15x+9-9 & =-6-9 \\\\ 15x & =-15 \\\\ \\frac{15x}{15} & =\\frac{-15}{15} \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince  $x=-1$ is different from $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=-\\frac{1}{3},$ we can accept it as a solution.\nThus, the solution is $x=-1.$"} :step/id #uuid "544b0bbe-7699-51f8-ab7e-6267f3303aae" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559940}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559940 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559812 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32f4c6f4-178d-5d4f-9cff-30346f3dab9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559144} {:answer/id #uuid "4a7a45de-e0ec-5bc5-ba26-4cf78d2fefc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559208} {:answer/id #uuid "889b7794-6975-5412-ab18-7cd2f1fda6ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559359} {:answer/id #uuid "b4f50d04-a640-5bf1-aa0f-e78901fe54e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559468} {:answer/id #uuid "fa1934be-efbe-592e-ba9b-fcbe1eb325e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559631}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559144} :answer-field/id #uuid "842edd8c-d49e-5de9-8d9e-73cf9af84901" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559717}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "600419ab-eff5-5dfa-9145-b6a621ba7867" :question/math-academy-id "q-51471" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{3x+12}=\\frac{2}{x+4}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that $x=-4$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nTo solve the equation, we first apply cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3x+12} & =\\frac{2}{x+4} \\\\ 2\\cdot (x+4) & =(3x+12)\\cdot 2 \\\\ 2x+8 & =6x+24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+8 & =6x+24 \\\\ 2x+8-24 & =6x+24-24 \\\\ 2x-16 & =6x \\\\ 2x-16-2x & =6x-2x \\\\ -16 & =4x \\\\ \\frac{-16}{4} & =\\frac{4x}{4} \\\\ -4 & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=-4$ cannot be a solution, the equation has no solutions."} :step/id #uuid "6e6e733b-eaf0-5101-bc15-75bd30fffd8b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559898}}] :activity/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing Binomials Using Cross-Multiplication" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558998}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084277} :activity/id #uuid "38aff8ec-fcf4-52b8-8557-cb2bcff71738" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048719 :topic/id #uuid "b8656b86-9f88-52e9-adb0-d3cc15fbb7cc" :topic/math-academy-id 755} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084277 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084484 :tutorial/content "The **lowest common multiple** (or $\\textrm{LCM}$) of two integers is the smallest multiple that's common to both numbers.\n\nTo find the lowest common multiple of two small numbers, we can list all multiples of the two numbers and identify the smallest multiple that they have in common.\n\nFor example, let's find the lowest common multiple of $4$ and $6.$ We start by listing all multiples of $4$ and $6\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & \\,\\to \\,4,\\,8,\\,\\boxed{\\text{[math]12[/math]}},\\,16,\\,20,\\,24,\\,… \\\\ 6 & \\,\\to \\,6,\\,\\boxed{\\text{[math]12[/math]}},\\,18,\\,24,\\,30,\\,36,\\,…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe smallest multiple in both lists is ![](images/92/9285d37c7b455395acb42a54f78348bedfd8c9a72ad63d6e6ab7c2426148ea0d.svg) So $12$ is our answer, and we can write\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(4,6)= 12.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The lowest common multiple is also sometimes called the *least* common multiple." :tutorial/id #uuid "b16f2464-2c19-5166-bd61-97043695afcb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5301 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "853eea8e-7e8c-5972-b2df-6a996d469c6a" :step/math-academy-id 33113 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084281}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084278 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083330 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083132 :question/id #uuid "f524667d-8cd2-5b91-a9e0-538fc64399e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-8869"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "384f8f04-972d-50cb-a023-dba29924242e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049086} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050170} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050212}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083690} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083711} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493920} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559830}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Lowest Common Multiple of Two Integers"} :step/id #uuid "0c8932d3-46c6-5cd6-941d-98887b4bfb0e" :step/math-academy-id 33116 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084280}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084279 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083331 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083133 :question/id #uuid "7c928766-9971-565a-b38b-5c97b2c3ee1d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8870"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c28dd57d-e6e5-5704-94a4-5f9bfb37f383" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083689} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493965}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Lowest Common Denominator of Two Fractions"} :step/id #uuid "764cac2a-73a8-581d-8ccd-4d665b951e2b" :step/math-academy-id 33118} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084280 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083332 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083134 :question/id #uuid "1373d2e3-fc83-56f2-b779-8f1e2d10efea" :question/math-academy-id "e-8874"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "746e99f3-561d-5212-ba2a-3d34b8bcdbee" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083479} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083712} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558531}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Lowest Common Multiple of Three Integers"} :step/id #uuid "7e705e91-32b9-5486-b6d0-c6ebfbc45c9b" :step/math-academy-id 33136 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084279}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084281 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084485 :tutorial/content "Now suppose that we want to find the lowest common multiple of two larger numbers. It would take a while to make long lists of multiples. Luckily, there is a faster method. \n\nLet's demonstrate by finding $\\textrm{LCM}(10,12).$ We start by writing the prime factorization of $10$ and $12$, writing any repeating factors as powers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =2\\cdot 5={2}^{1}\\cdot {5}^{1} \\\\ 12 & =2\\cdot 2\\cdot 3={2}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write down each unique prime factor that appears in either of our decompositions. In our case, the unique prime factors are \n\n$$\n{\\color{red}2}, \\qquad {\\color{red}3}, \\qquad {\\color{red}5}.\n$$\n\nNow, for each number in our list, we look at *both* prime factorizations and we take the *largest* power shown for each prime factor:\n\n- The factors corresponding to ${\\color{red}2}$ are ${\\color{red}2}^{\\color{blue}1}$ and ${\\color{red}2}^{\\color{blue}2}.$ Of these, the larger power is ${\\color{red}2}^{\\color{blue}2}.$\n\n- There is only one factor corresponding to ${\\color{red}3}.$ It is ${\\color{red}3}^{\\color{blue}1}.$\n\n- There is only one factor corresponding to ${\\color{red}5}.$ It is ${\\color{red}5}^{\\color{blue}1}.$\n\nFinally, we multiply the largest powers of each factor together, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{LCM}(10,12) & ={2}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{1}\\cdot {5}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "de44c642-c9d2-5c1d-8b59-8781a99559ef" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5489 :tutorial/title "Finding the Lowest Common Multiple of Two Larger Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "44919f35-fd65-50af-b639-7d5eb83ab3b0" :step/math-academy-id 34200 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084278}}] :activity/title "Finding Lowest Common Multiples Using Prime Factorization" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186083445}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111539} :activity/id #uuid "91d80949-4bd6-531f-a855-c98a58ea2f32" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048764 :topic/id #uuid "bce11f5d-4b21-5819-b55b-282994760aee" :topic/math-academy-id 1511} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111539 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111922 :tutorial/content "Two angles are **supplementary** if their measures add up to $180^\\circ.$\n\n![](images/88/886f1ce19ad595b470d5ad13943b780bd5a22ee737908d9554e97bd08c9ce985.png)\n\nThese angles are supplementary because the sum of their measures is $180^\\circ{:}$\n\n$$\n105^\\circ +75^\\circ = 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nA **linear pair** is a pair of adjacent angles formed when two lines intersect. The angles shown above form a linear pair.\n\nThe **linear pair postulate** states the following:\n\nIf two angles form a linear pair, then they are supplementary.\n\nThe converse is *not* true. That is, if two angles are supplementary, they do not necessarily form a linear pair.\n\nTo see why, consider the diagram shown below:\n\n![](images/29/29ff0dfefb0b535342f4e8060b37fb2404983fc150681bb02d18fa6e5b00f899.png)\n\nAlthough the angles are supplementary (the sum of their measures is $120^\\circ + 60^\\circ = 180^\\circ$), they do not form a linear pair of angles since they are not adjacent." :tutorial/id #uuid "ea9626ef-f56f-5668-8f47-81a9f68c9db4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9518 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "34d571bc-0e65-56b1-80d0-82208a4c2869" :step/math-academy-id 193869 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111540}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111540 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110627 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110357 :question/id #uuid "4ebed10c-0a87-528a-ba18-67d960ba7692" :question/math-academy-id "e-15641"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "931f7270-ec55-5c42-8c1b-6c7988f6c921" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048723} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050494}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111176} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499469} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499474} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499519} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503660} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503738}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Supplementary Angles"} :step/id #uuid "677af1bc-75a9-505c-baf3-c267afccb1b8" :step/math-academy-id 193870 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111541}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111541 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110628 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110358 :question/id #uuid "ba553f7b-1750-5ffb-b6f8-81ad141368a8" :question/math-academy-id "e-15642"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ba5d861-c6bf-57df-aa00-7034aeb5e1ac" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048725}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503697} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503702} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503724} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503754}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Supplementary Angles"} :step/id #uuid "1635a868-152f-5d33-a98e-b081e4cab5fa" :step/math-academy-id 193873 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111542}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111542 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110629 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110355 :question/id #uuid "62cec20b-28d4-5f08-9201-2d32bb741f1b" :question/math-academy-id "e-15141"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2c7eec3a-50e3-5550-9e1e-def64ca9642a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049939} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050452}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110997} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503670} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503751}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Supplementary Angles From a Description"} :step/id #uuid "ae5c4a50-4790-50a4-8623-989e8ec2863b" :step/math-academy-id 193876 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111543}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111543 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110630 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110356 :question/id #uuid "39e78adf-4714-5461-b3a4-c619019df41a" :question/math-academy-id "e-15142"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e6e34d9-29a2-55ed-9d28-258cbfd91600" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050613}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503652} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503713} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511552}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Quantity Using Supplementary Angles"} :step/id #uuid "fbb2518f-7e11-56ac-abf6-3669b2e06083" :step/math-academy-id 193879}] :activity/title "Supplementary Angles" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110866}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148270} :activity/id #uuid "66824b85-374f-5412-9f2f-47a7d45ad385" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048995 :topic/id #uuid "d1edd866-fc11-5971-b454-c205e7356f7f" :topic/math-academy-id 2464} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148270 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148795 :tutorial/content "We can use our knowledge of solving exponential equations to obtain information about a system that follows an exponential growth model.\n\nFor example, suppose that the revenue $R(t),$ in millions of dollars, of a rapidly growing startup company is given by the function\n\n$$\nR(t) = 2\\cdot 3^t,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the number of years since the company was incorporated. How long will it take for the company's revenue to reach $\\[math]18$ million?\n\nTo find the solution to this problem, we need to solve the equation $R(t) = 18.$ This gives us the equation\n\n$$\n2\\cdot 3^t = 18.\n$$\n\nTo solve this equation, we first divide both sides by $2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot {3}^{t} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot {3}^{t}}{2} & =\\frac{18}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}\\cdot {3}^{t}}{\\cancel{2}} & =9 \\\\ {3}^{t} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we notice that $9$ is a power of $3,$ because $9=3^2.$ So we get\n\n$$\n3^{\\color{blue}t} = 3^{\\color{blue}2}.\n$$\n\nThe bases of both exponents are equal, so the powers must also be equal. Therefore, our solution is\n\n$$\nt = {\\color{blue}2}.\n$$\n\nWe conclude that it will take the company $\\color{blue}2$ years to reach a revenue of $\\[/math]18$ million." :tutorial/id #uuid "35ba03ad-41cc-5f76-9618-f78f0e3c0529" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8849 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "87338de0-96ec-5a69-a9d2-940674b36916" :step/math-academy-id 196865 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148271}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148271 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147421 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147176 :question/id #uuid "94d6edd2-52f9-581a-84f0-3003ccbaadd8" :question/math-academy-id "e-14266"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7328749-7ecf-5040-9d18-e9274187d485" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050511}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147892} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497357}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Time"} :step/id #uuid "7f05b5aa-3eba-5e22-981c-ba97b773c331" :step/math-academy-id 196866 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148272}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148272 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147422 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147177 :question/id #uuid "4a2c8602-04d5-5ec7-846f-2647249994fe" :question/math-academy-id "e-14267"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "363026fc-b4ac-5b35-8825-717a113df12b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048302}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147731} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147750} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497384}] :knowledge-point/title "Creating a Model and Solving for an Unknown Time"} :step/id #uuid "76f4ea70-5d3e-59c6-ab03-6093273dafcf" :step/math-academy-id 196869 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148273}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148273 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147423 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147178 :question/id #uuid "9759efbf-fdb7-595c-822d-85c6b5f3549e" :question/math-academy-id "e-14271"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "08d13ef7-5569-5cc4-9692-9ed99a3be3e8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147912} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497341} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497363}] :knowledge-point/title "Creating and Solving a Model Given Some Percentage Growth"} :step/id #uuid "07e18a18-e0f7-514d-9a1d-5e49ae4b0b94" :step/math-academy-id 196872 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148274}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148274 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148796 :tutorial/content "We often need to know the system's state at $t=0$ when using an exponential growth model.\n\nFor example, suppose that the number of users $N(t)$ of a new phone app can be modeled by the function\n\n$$\nN(t) = N_0(1.2)^t,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ represents the time, in weeks, since the app launched, and $N_0$ is a constant. If there are $5\\,000$ users $5$ weeks after launch, can we determine how many users the app had when it launched?\n\nHere, $N_0$ represents the initial number of users, and this is what we're trying to find.\n\nFrom the given information, we know that $N(5) = 5\\,000,$ So let's plug this into our function:\n\n$$\n5\\,000 = N_0(1.2)^5\n$$\n\nTo solve this equation, we can divide both sides by $(1.2)^5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5\\,000}{(1.2)^{5}} & =\\frac{{N}_{0}(1.2)^{5}}{(1.2)^{5}} \\\\ \\frac{5\\,000}{(1.2)^{5}} & =\\frac{{N}_{0}\\cancel{(1.2)^{5}}}{\\cancel{(1.2)^{5}}} \\\\ \\frac{5\\,000}{(1.2)^{5}} & ={N}_{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's flip the equation around to make it easier to work with. \n\n$$\nN_0 = \\dfrac{5\\,000}{(1.2)^5}\n$$\n\nWe can now work out $N_0$ using a calculator. We find that\n\n$$\nN_0\\approx 2\\,009.\n$$\n\nWe have rounded to the nearest integer since $N_0$ refers to a number of people.\n\nTherefore, when it launched, the app had $2\\,009$ users." :tutorial/id #uuid "ae3d753f-4a2d-55b0-9fb7-a3f746f66018" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8854 :tutorial/title "Unknown Initial Values"} :step/id #uuid "da8a83fe-da24-58c8-be84-aad5b70f01a0" :step/math-academy-id 196875 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148275}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148275 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147424 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147179 :question/id #uuid "07a30fba-ad4b-56a0-b6ce-21941d106b38" :question/math-academy-id "e-14276"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2c127787-8a31-524b-8b7d-68c15b714335" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147747} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147754} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497330} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497382}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Initial Value"} :step/id #uuid "20edaa81-4eeb-5d4b-98a7-837cb86e910d" :step/math-academy-id 196876}] :activity/title "Solving Exponential Growth Problems" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147664}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180320} :activity/id #uuid "a4c8ca08-bd22-5363-926f-e23513ac4154" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049201 :topic/id #uuid "e2f84ebe-3953-57c1-b57f-978988ee6101" :topic/math-academy-id 3725} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180320 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180783 :tutorial/content "To determine the slope of a line that is given in standard form, we need to convert the equation to slope-intercept form.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's calculate the slope of the following line:\n\n$$\n2x + y = 8\n$$\n\nTo write this equation in slope-intercept form, we solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+y & =8 \\\\ 2x+y-2x & =8-2x \\\\ y & =8-2x \\\\ y & =-2x+8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the slope-intercept equation, we can tell that the slope of the line is $m=-2.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3550ae2f-df69-52cc-b33a-14e8f2bb2d8a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8697 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "72c1429e-a7f2-51eb-b430-aeb492262ee7" :step/math-academy-id 138017 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180321}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180321 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179631 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179474 :question/id #uuid "9b0cfd54-9310-59e6-91cb-5b18413ae7b3" :question/math-academy-id "e-13979"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48f52c24-cd5a-5701-8ec2-1d833e87a25f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049840}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180131} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501175} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501205}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of a Line Given Standard Form"} :step/id #uuid "fd4133a0-8060-545f-b970-74364bd3c8af" :step/math-academy-id 138018 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180322}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180322 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180784 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find the $x$- and $y$-intercepts of the following line:\n\n$$\n2x + y = 8\n$$\n\nLet's find each of them in turn.\n\n- To find the $x$-intercept, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+(0) & =8 \\\\ 2x & =8 \\\\ \\frac{2x}{2} & =\\frac{8}{2} \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line intersects the $x$-axis at the point $(4,0).$\n\n- To find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation and solve for $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)+y & =8 \\\\ y & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line intersects the $y$-axis at the point $(0,8).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c500072d-6462-54c2-8c16-3c8f68bdbbfe" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8699 :tutorial/title "Determining Intercepts of Lines Given in Standard Form"} :step/id #uuid "ba8eac6e-a6b0-5050-a5e7-2508d56d4806" :step/math-academy-id 138022 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180323}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180323 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179632 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179475 :question/id #uuid "d1904d74-7f31-5211-b7d4-88f4f5042d64" :question/math-academy-id "e-13982"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd8533c7-7573-5917-b347-ce72d26a7168" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180122} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501176} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501240}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the X-Intercept of a Line"} :step/id #uuid "8393e8f1-d3a4-51f5-bea6-a2c3546128e8" :step/math-academy-id 138023 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180324}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180324 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179633 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179476 :question/id #uuid "cbfa4dda-a399-5450-bacd-7681aebceede" :question/math-academy-id "e-13986"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee8d9c8a-51c2-56b4-8006-e768bb7fa128" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180123} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180124} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501136} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501237}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Y-Intercept of a Line"} :step/id #uuid "3a721917-d440-59e3-a3b3-441903fcc6b5" :step/math-academy-id 138026}] :activity/title "Properties of Lines Given in Standard Form" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179876}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139330} :activity/id #uuid "a31e6d9a-a5fd-5a92-8bfe-ccf7c139d6c2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049369 :topic/id #uuid "ef52c4c1-b1f3-57d2-9328-5e8e0f981eaa" :topic/math-academy-id 2273} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139330 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139718 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we're making a juice drink with a fixed amount of pure orange and the rest water. \n\nThe more water that we add, the less that we'll be able to taste the orange. \n\nMore precisely, the concentration of pure orange $y$ (in grams per liter) varies **inversely** with the volume $x$ (in liters) of water added. We can express this idea using an **inverse variation equation** as follows:\n\n$$\ny= \\dfrac{k}{x}\n$$\n\nThe constant $k$ is called the **constant of variation** or **constant of proportionality.**\n\nNotice that:\n\n- If $x$ is small (i.e., there is not much water), then $y$ is large (i.e., there is a high concentration of orange).\n\n- If $x$ is large (i.e., there is lots of water), then $y$ is small (i.e., there is a low concentration of orange).\n\nTo find the constant of variation $k$, we need to plug in some data. \n\nFor example, suppose we know that the concentration is $y = 200\\, \\textrm{g/L}$ when $x = 4\\,\\textrm L$ of water has been added. Plugging this into our relationship and solving for $k,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{k}{x} \\\\ 200 & =\\frac{k}{4} \\\\ 200\\cdot 4 & =k \\\\ 800 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the relationship between $y$ and $x$ is\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac {800} x.\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that we can rewrite the above relationship as\n\n$$\nxy = 800.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "119903a2-f337-5919-9085-bff5477e0375" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8754 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b9c91899-d231-53dd-a5ef-3ff5dda6d1ff" :step/math-academy-id 191249 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139331}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139331 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138413 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138169 :question/id #uuid "8a12b2fd-6361-59ef-8378-6c900a1ab47b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14089"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d9375c5-8905-5a25-8585-47a1861238c0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050613}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138751} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138820} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498521} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498540} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498567}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Inverse Variation Equation Given One Data Point"} :step/id #uuid "7536e83b-56d0-53cd-8fc7-4e96012da74b" :step/math-academy-id 191250 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139332}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139332 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138414 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138170 :question/id #uuid "a2d2a2a1-da7b-5106-8347-3a4f71ddd619" :question/math-academy-id "e-14090"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e239c25-66f9-5ea7-ba3c-5599966cd89b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051029}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138663} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138744} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498497} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498500} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518113}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Second Data Point Given Another Data Point"} :step/id #uuid "c2dcaebf-65a1-5edf-9cd3-fd75eb90fc4b" :step/math-academy-id 191254 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139333}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139333 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139720 :tutorial/content "If  $y$ varies inversely with  $x,$ then we can also say that $y$ is **inversely proportional** to $x.$ We can express this mathematically as\n\n$$\ny \\propto \\dfrac{1}{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\propto$ is the proportionality symbol.\n\nThe following statements are equivalent:\n\n- $y = \\dfrac{k}{x}$ for some constant $k$\n\n- $y$ varies inversely with $x$\n\n- $y$ is inversely proportional to $x$\n\n- $y \\propto \\dfrac{1}{x}$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5b87278f-f657-5fcd-a760-5c4b53c783b4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8756 :tutorial/title "Alternative Formulations"} :step/id #uuid "53ccf363-f93a-5e86-87e9-b5eba1af0a5c" :step/math-academy-id 191258 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139334}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139334 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138415 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138172 :question/id #uuid "80333f8b-9aff-5f31-8301-a8708d531a22" :question/math-academy-id "e-14092"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c4d2228-2456-536c-87f5-c05f03ac1532" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050383}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138825} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138827} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498519} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518108}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Table Represents an Inverse Variation"} :step/id #uuid "a384bde8-822b-5921-a245-47c8bf6118d6" :step/math-academy-id 191259 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139335}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139335 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138416 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138173 :question/id #uuid "9c285374-0465-52ed-a283-c469e123d167" :question/math-academy-id "e-14094"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "482e3ce6-1938-5bbe-a0d3-2c44213c7746" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048993}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138745} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138746} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498536} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518200}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Inverse Variation Equation Given a Graph"} :step/id #uuid "3663f818-19c8-5a2e-a534-3e4acaee2552" :step/math-academy-id 191264}] :activity/title "Inverse Variation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186138599}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518308} :activity/id #uuid "2fbef69a-88c5-5d96-acb5-ba687d9fd119" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049369 :topic/id #uuid "ef52c4c1-b1f3-57d2-9328-5e8e0f981eaa" :topic/math-academy-id 2273} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518249 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518200 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08ff8e18-e1e0-569c-b239-ea2d323c2f96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517506} {:answer/id #uuid "2c3f1cdb-62a7-5446-bfc0-9c5b8ecff4dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517570} {:answer/id #uuid "37c06ed4-4dac-5a25-bb1d-90e1da0780f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517598} {:answer/id #uuid "3d5ad2d6-7a2b-58fa-9b9e-b5cfbe4b0f29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517610} {:answer/id #uuid "72cc3a7f-05b3-50d0-8c4f-4aedc32143ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517708}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517708} :answer-field/id #uuid "d155659c-c17e-5eca-b3d0-23cecfde70bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518069}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cf708341-904e-56d2-b929-e74c203d19a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-115589" :question/problem "![](images/96/9674072fe2d9f831635dfd2994b97ea6e9603dd576586f80e4b1b2e6a2373564.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the relationship between $x$ and $y.$ Given that $y$ is inversely proportional to $x,$ find the value of $a.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $y$ varies inversely with $x,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{k}{x}.\n$$\n\nIn the graph, we notice a point with $y=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=5.$ We substitute this data into the equation to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{k}{5} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5 & =k \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse variation equation that relates $y$ and $x$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(\\frac{5}{2})}{x}=\\frac{5}{2x}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we also have that $y=\\frac{5}{2}$ when $x=a.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2} & =\\frac{5}{2a} \\\\ 10a & =10 \\\\ a & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "47c6ba42-9469-510d-8375-d74fdf459167" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518273}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518273 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518113 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1e2c107-9d5e-504f-af80-17ffca1d2a08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517858}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517858} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7f79e97-d630-5908-a05a-ee3b6a7eb854" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518075}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1c37fb77-7c99-58f3-b32e-45223dbee74b" :question/math-academy-id "q-217248" :question/problem "Suppose that $y$ varies inversely with $x$ and that $y=\\frac{3}{4}$ when $x=12.$ Then, if $x=\\frac{1}{3},$ $y=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Since $y$ varies inversely with $x,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{k}{x}.\n$$\n\nWe are told that $y=\\frac{3}{4}$ when $x=12.$ We substitute this data into the equation to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4} & =\\frac{k}{12} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}\\cdot 12 & =k \\\\ 9 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the inverse variation is given by \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{9}{x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the value of $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ into this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{9}{(\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =9÷(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =9\\cdot (\\frac{3}{1}) \\\\  & =27.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "946d1bf9-981b-5a6b-afb7-e0f580f11f36"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518308 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518108 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1039e812-9a88-5b9b-b551-269c2393b23c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$y$ is inversely proportional to $x$ with a constant of variation $k=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517522} {:answer/id #uuid "763b9117-6856-5174-865d-697d35865dd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$y$ does not vary inversely with $x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517712} {:answer/id #uuid "aef8d086-e3c3-5c2f-b73f-df730fbb213a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$y$ is inversely proportional to $x$ with a constant of variation $k=10$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517817} {:answer/id #uuid "b9b86d47-b504-5381-aaca-f8c9b8bcb39f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$y$ is inversely proportional to $x$ with a constant of variation $k=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517837} {:answer/id #uuid "f07b6d5a-a5a6-55bc-bf86-f6c9b061a44a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$y$ is inversely proportional to $x$ with a constant of variation $k=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517948}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517817} :answer-field/id #uuid "f89a1044-5822-5030-9b03-184a93ebec0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "11ab4bcc-ff94-5b73-9790-5edd61c08193" :question/math-academy-id "q-115488" :question/problem "Consider the table below. Which of the following is a true statement about the relationship between $x$ and $y?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $0.5$ | $20$ |\n| $2$ | $5$ |\n| $4$ | $2.5$ |" :question/worked-solution "If $y$ varies inversely with $x,$ then we must have\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{k}{x}.\n$$\n\nfor some constant $k.$ Note that we can rewrite the above equation as follows.\n\n$$\nx\\cdot y=k\n$$\n\nSo, we will compute $x\\cdot y=k$ for each row in the table. If we get the same value of $k$ for each row, then we can conclude that $y$ is inversely proportional to $x.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $x\\cdot y=k$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $0.5$ | $20$ | $0.5\\cdot 20=10$ |\n| $2$ | $5$ | $2\\cdot 5=10$ |\n| $4$ | $2.5$ | $4\\cdot 2.5=10$ |\n\nThe results are all the same: in each row, we have $k=10.$ Therefore, $y$ varies inversely with $x,$ and the constant of variation is $k=10.$"} :step/id #uuid "e3cb0b4d-15dc-5baa-9658-0e5055d4371c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518249}}] :activity/title "Inverse Variation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517480}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129928} :activity/id #uuid "4c188df7-e524-54c2-8a5d-b2f16e8dfb77" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049472 :topic/id #uuid "f7e72507-2c32-5978-b2b5-175e533dfdf0" :topic/math-academy-id 2022} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129928 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130482 :tutorial/content "The **roots** of a function are the values of the input $x$ for which the function equals zero. For this reason, roots are sometimes also called **zeros**.\n\nAlgebraically, the roots of a function are the solutions for $x$ in the following equation:\n\n$$\nf(x)=0\n$$\n\nFor example, if $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{2}x+1,$ then we can find the roots by solving the equation $f(x) = 0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x+1 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x & =-1 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, this function has a single root: $x=-2.$\n\nGraphically, the roots of a function are the $x$-intercepts. For example, for the graph of $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{2}x+1$ shown below, we can see that the function intercepts the $x$-axis at $x=-2.$\n\n![](images/a7/a75e8a0ae40c5cc940f9fe2d9194b0dcb5c6db2742e87a6d903dc15c0b970e99.png)\n\nWhen shown on a graph, the roots of a function are also called the $\\boldsymbol x$**-intercepts.**" :tutorial/id #uuid "37a5be36-f2b1-5ebb-91b6-98ccbc9babaa" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8802 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "14413a03-0cc3-58a8-9a8d-a19f98f83fa5" :step/math-academy-id 146046 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129929}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129929 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129106 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128873 :question/id #uuid "c1e36290-8271-5508-9bf8-cf15a3907f75" :question/math-academy-id "e-14174"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "82f5682f-73b8-5c0a-b7d3-3336200843a0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048838}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129819} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503649} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503696}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Function Given Its Graph"} :step/id #uuid "b4ec4b62-fcff-5a84-8aff-d4e6e77464a0" :step/math-academy-id 146047 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129930}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129930 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129107 :knowledge-point/canonical-e