g the Roots Given a Function Definition"} :step/id #uuid "459f52ce-a337-5d29-918d-c439315a2395" :step/math-academy-id 146056}] :activity/title "The Roots of a Function" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129364}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557405} :activity/id #uuid "ad1504c9-2cb0-5fbd-8072-a67bb0a595b0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049472 :topic/id #uuid "f7e72507-2c32-5978-b2b5-175e533dfdf0" :topic/math-academy-id 2022} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557361 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557228 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "989608c8-9218-5c75-88f5-8e4e4b9c8cf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$40$ and $70$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556796} {:answer/id #uuid "9b5ae992-2c54-5206-802e-b1b2ca2238ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$10$ and $80$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556809} {:answer/id #uuid "aa81c40a-29e9-5b90-aa31-f1b514f34e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$50$ and $60$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556851} {:answer/id #uuid "c2dbdc2f-39d6-538b-9826-8f355d259ab3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$20$ and $50$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556901} {:answer/id #uuid "d463ad01-7754-59b7-9f2e-30cf3c021ff4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$30$ and $60$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556946}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556796} :answer-field/id #uuid "112eb8e3-a60e-524c-8724-a81064b58bcb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557073}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "30380421-4837-53f2-affd-65b4ad08f0c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-82668" :question/problem "If $g(x)$ is given by the table shown below, what are its roots?\n\n| $x$ | $10$ | $20$ | $30$ | $40$ | $50$ | $60$ | $70$ | $80$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(x)$ | $5$ | $15$ | $25$ | $0$ | $-15$ | $-25$ | $0$ | $5$ |" :question/worked-solution "The roots of the function are the values of $x$ such that $g(x)=0.$ In the given table, we see that $g(40)=0$ and $g(70)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the roots of the function are $x=40$ and $x=70.$"} :step/id #uuid "5de8eb33-d802-54e5-aa96-cdcc2203cde7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557375}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557375 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557329 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ed1228a-7373-5a96-877e-e5c7fe47067d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "| $x$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $2$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $5$ | $10$ | $12$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556605} {:answer/id #uuid "505f7fd0-1497-5427-8ea5-504d5959d49b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "| $x$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $3$ | $2$ | $1$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556611} {:answer/id #uuid "a3f69b99-1c6d-5613-9d2e-27ffb7439256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "| $x$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $-4$ | $-5$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556830} {:answer/id #uuid "a88c84c6-3f5e-5722-b013-555a318561ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "| $x$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-3$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556848} {:answer/id #uuid "fbcb6e52-0b1e-5682-8a11-a9bdab6b8164" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "| $x$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $4$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $-5$ | $-7$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557053}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557053} :answer-field/id #uuid "03f14d9d-05ea-5eba-9f15-106f65b248f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557063}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f7198a9b-72ee-54fc-b7ac-cda83b608de8" :question/math-academy-id "q-82670" :question/problem "Which of the following tables describes a function that has $0$ as a root?" :question/worked-solution "The number $0$ is a root of $f(x)$ if $f(0)=0.$ Of the given tables, only the following table has this property:\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $4$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $-5$ | $-7$ |"} :step/id #uuid "86ef8295-2490-501a-9c7c-8f68fdb3f21a"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557405 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557303 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cd9eaf3-ed30-5541-af86-8a86b83910ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$0$ and $\\frac{1}{7}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556496} {:answer/id #uuid "2e59e379-49a8-54d1-a572-f41f95712c03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x)$ has no roots" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556529} {:answer/id #uuid "6e2ccad8-734d-582e-b7c7-0ed5544f1b87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{7}$ and $\\frac{1}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556683} {:answer/id #uuid "8790a791-e8bb-5896-87ed-e82e9ab0674d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556749} {:answer/id #uuid "956e4771-73f0-5b6f-980d-af1813caa099" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-4$ and $-\\frac{1}{9}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556789}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556749} :answer-field/id #uuid "e84544a2-76c0-5219-9d3a-4068e68e816b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557195}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c306bf08-706c-5a8c-b907-b249d9ec68eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-37888" :question/problem "![](images/ef/ef42744182deb0d83704b13fba2324b92df197151c3566de01bb75a202e836cc.png)\n\nWhat are the roots of the function $f(x)$ defined by the mapping diagram shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The roots of the function are the values of $x$ such that $f(x)=0.$ In the given mapping diagram, we see that $f(\\frac{1}{3})=0.$\n\nTherefore, the only root of the function is $x=\\frac{1}{3}.$"} :step/id #uuid "efc74aa4-b0b2-5917-86c7-5993bf2370b5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557361}}] :activity/title "The Roots of a Function" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556417}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148535} :activity/id #uuid "3903e573-e595-5f4d-8dbd-a1c1b28ab808" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050386 :topic/id #uuid "44bfd7a4-62f3-56cf-89cb-1d3f0e772674" :topic/math-academy-id 2456} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148535 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148781 :tutorial/content "To find a part given a percent and whole, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percent} \\times \\textrm{whole} = \\textrm{part}\n$$\n\nHowever, we sometimes want to find a whole given a part and a percent. To do this, we can use the relationship between multiplication and division to rewrite the above formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{whole} = \\textrm{part} \\div \\textrm{percent}\n$$\n\nLet's use this formula to find the missing number in the following statement:\n![](images/08/082c6ac53fa8de85b739dde0611943e92cb09d74d07378d6a33043d4b231e88d.svg)\n\nIn this case, the part is $540$, and the percent is $30\\%.$ \n\nWe can express $30\\%$ as a fraction as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{30}{100} = \\dfrac{30\\div 10}{100\\div 10} = \\dfrac{3}{10}\n$$\n\nPutting these numbers into our formula for the whole, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{whole} & =\\text{part}÷\\text{percent} \\\\  & =540÷30% \\\\  & =540÷\\frac{3}{10} \\\\  & =540\\times \\frac{10}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{540}{3}\\times 10 \\\\  & =180\\times 10 \\\\  & =1,800\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $1,800.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "69026176-778a-58ea-8ccf-185968679bd3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8170 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e956a0b5-6283-59e2-b00b-6fff43cb6aaf" :step/math-academy-id 72289 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148536}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148536 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147575 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147148 :question/id #uuid "e650884c-e858-5213-8ff4-a39f9358edc0" :question/math-academy-id "e-13066"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb8b97e4-704c-5b13-97c7-6eb97aca392f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049616} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050716}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501207}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Total Given a Part and a Multiple of Ten Percent"} :step/id #uuid "20f09f61-d500-5013-be0d-df259b534912" :step/math-academy-id 72290 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148537}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148537 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147576 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147149 :question/id #uuid "4bfda07b-18fd-541a-a627-ef7f1e47f41a" :question/math-academy-id "e-13067"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "99c69dea-ec5d-525d-ae0f-da30e342fe63" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147879} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501162} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501211} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562391}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Total Given a Part and a Percentage"} :step/id #uuid "58253af5-26fc-5261-b61b-97788f445428" :step/math-academy-id 72293 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148538}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148538 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147577 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147150 :question/id #uuid "bd7aaf49-0da5-53c8-a5ee-863523548957" :question/math-academy-id "e-13068"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1670b7f6-fdee-5358-b145-9254e2a4ba50" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147881} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501210}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Total Given a Part and a Percentage: Word Problems"} :step/id #uuid "9f86a1a0-8db6-5c95-9948-5487d96d589a" :step/math-academy-id 72296}] :activity/title "Finding a Total Given a Part and a Percentage" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147657}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116390} :activity/id #uuid "e244395f-15ed-5f0d-a0d8-1d032e7f6961" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050410 :topic/id #uuid "46ce73a1-ea0d-553e-b5fc-67d8d59a64f8" :topic/math-academy-id 1601} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116095 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115224 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115054 :question/id #uuid "9c478978-dbb7-53c7-b076-c763b4d89c19" :question/math-academy-id "e-4268"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "676e3d29-f6fa-5fe4-aae4-e9c0e974ee74" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488224}] :knowledge-point/title "Ordering the Angles of a Triangle by Size Given the Sides"} :step/id #uuid "da9b45ae-e6d0-5a16-9c2a-a707a86b6bbf" :step/math-academy-id 15670} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116390 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116446 :tutorial/content "Triangles can be classified by sides or by angles.\n\nFirst, let's consider the classifications by sides. We have three groups of triangles:\n\n- **Equilateral** triangles, where all sides have the same length.\n\n- **Isosceles** triangles, where only two sides are congruent. The congruent sides are called the **legs** of the triangle, and the third side is called the **base**.\n\n- **Scalene** triangles, where all sides have different measures.\n\n![](images/dc/dc38034d6c202b5168fc8184511a839eb41af206eb733e71e5f5c2fa970c0e33.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "04b4a44f-3943-5a08-9132-ec500b7ff786" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2224 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2105e8d3-69ba-53f8-b170-b27097df9c59" :step/math-academy-id 9127 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116392}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116391 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115406 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114982 :question/id #uuid "7dd8ef62-3aaf-5db9-9d85-6953f0520e8d" :question/math-academy-id "e-2713"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b19a236-cd8f-52be-9ed2-24581059f7f4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049584}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115539} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115911} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488211}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Triangle Based on Its Sides"} :step/id #uuid "b726def9-901b-503a-8593-201dafc3ad42" :step/math-academy-id 9133 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116393}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116392 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116447 :tutorial/content "Now, let's consider classifying triangles by angles. We have three groups of triangles.\n\n- In an **acute** triangle, all three angles are acute.\n\n- A triangle with a right angle is called a **right** triangle.\n\n- A triangle with an obtuse angle is called an **obtuse** triangle.\n\n![](images/f1/f1c28d1f4b1ec7a93f1778e63be3727aa227e8eea5320eb7d5579c697e709429.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "c58ae206-f2dc-54ff-8d30-951635f040c4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2228 :tutorial/title "Classifying Triangles Based on Angles"} :step/id #uuid "93db5f60-a5c9-58e2-9ec2-2983e7914914" :step/math-academy-id 9135 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116391}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116393 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115407 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114983 :question/id #uuid "503325cd-6b72-5216-999f-6601f54aa1b1" :question/math-academy-id "e-2716"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "391481e0-3bd2-570f-8948-5618d8eb40c4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049949}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115540} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115639} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488232}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Triangle Based on Its Angles"} :step/id #uuid "3f9f27e3-19a0-5fd1-8583-922e58bff6de" :step/math-academy-id 9136 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116394}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116394 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116448 :tutorial/content "Consider the following scalene triangle, where $AB=5$, $BC=7,$ and $AC=6$ units, respectively.\n\n![](images/57/57be69075640f742ffe6c004515485c4f479347f3b8b523272778948935048df.png)\n\nLooking at the angles, it's hard to tell which angle is the largest.\n\nHowever, since we know the sides, there is a simple rule that we can use to determine the relative size of the angles:\n\nLonger sides are opposite to larger angles and vice versa.\n\nApplying this rule to $\\triangle{ABC}$ depicted above, the longest side is $\\overline{BC}.$ Therefore, the largest angle is the angle opposite to $\\overline{BC},$ which is $\\color{red}\\angle{A}.$\n\n![](images/82/82d9ac6d61f285f3557d2dd0dda826803ffae306c5f9a927a422b3b953f262f4.png)\n\nSimilarly,\n\n- the mid-sized side ($\\overline{AC}$) is opposite to the mid-sized angle ($\\angle B$), and\n\n- the shortest side ($\\overline{AB}$) is opposite to the smallest angle ($\\angle C$).\n\nSo, from least to greatest, the angles of the triangle are ordered as follows:\n\n$$\nm\\angle{C} < m\\angle{B} < m\\angle{A}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This rule works in reverse too. If you know the biggest angle of a triangle, the longest side is always opposite to this angle." :tutorial/id #uuid "d464a407-72e1-515b-876a-cb5b0dd3b06f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2229 :tutorial/title "Relationships Between Sides and Interior Angles of a Triangle"} :step/id #uuid "a22f2d5b-d95d-5243-8574-99ba246bfda2" :step/math-academy-id 9141 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116395}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116395 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115408 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114984 :question/id #uuid "cbac3c76-7a3e-5a86-8e03-667874fe97f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-2719"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3360e34f-afe6-5f9e-be39-91e2d51ba353" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115897} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115900} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488212} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550012}] :knowledge-point/title "Ordering the Sides of a Triangle by Size Given the Interior Angles"} :step/id #uuid "0db57a5e-6e16-5eaa-adcb-b67a00c827c4" :step/math-academy-id 9148 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116095}}] :activity/title "Classifying Triangles" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186115429}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139556} :activity/id #uuid "522ceb8a-6a60-5c1f-99d9-c6421e013ec3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050505 :topic/id #uuid "4ed6acfb-fbd7-5e57-abcc-691c74600c49" :topic/math-academy-id 2278} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139556 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139635 :tutorial/content "Let's look at the three points $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ below. \n\n![](images/95/956f9b2c979f06897b09119b089ced903e095d72136e64b9ea52e946fba41544.png)\n\nAs indicated in the diagram, the distances between the points are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB=4\\,cm,\\,BC=2\\,cm,\\,AC=6\\,cm.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCan we be sure that the points are collinear? To answer this, we use the **segment addition postulate**.\n\nThe postulate states the following: \n\n*Three points are collinear if and only if the largest distance between two of them is equal to the sum of the other two distances.*\n\nHere, the largest distance is $AC,$ so the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ are collinear *if and only if*\n\n$$\nAC=AB + BC.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information $AC=6\\,\\mathrm{cm},$ $AB=4\\,\\mathrm{cm},$ and $BC=2\\,\\mathrm{cm}$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =AB+BC \\\\ 6 & =4+2 \\\\ 6 & =6.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a true statement, so the three points $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ are indeed collinear.\n\n**Note:** The postulate works in both directions:\n\n- If the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ are collinear then $AC= AB + BC.$\n\n- If $AC= AB + BC$ then the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ are collinear." :tutorial/id #uuid "0a460529-da55-5fcc-a207-e4ae6a47f12d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2168 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "11ec6bb7-b9b3-51ea-b1b2-5f6a0a7b75b9" :step/math-academy-id 8720 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139557}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139557 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139636 :tutorial/content "Given two of the three distances between three collinear points, we can calculate the third distance using the segment addition postulate. \n\nTo demonstrate, consider the collinear points $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ below.\n\n![](images/3b/3b1bc383e8d570f9dc33346e6f4ae04377e3c5c4c0d0e53abe07b0aacd9bb3e6.png)\n\nWe are given two of the distances:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB=3\\,cm,\\,AC=5\\,cm\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the third distance, $BC,$ using the segment addition postulate.\n\nFirst, we state the equation and substitute the known quantities $AB=3$ and $AC=5,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =AB+BC \\\\ 5 & =3+BC\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for $BC$ by subtracting $3$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =3+BC \\\\ BC & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance between the points $B$ and $C$ is $2\\,\\textrm{cm}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "92286c39-6af9-5ff0-b2e9-fa44fec8f732" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2169 :tutorial/title "Calculating the Distance Between Collinear Points"} :step/id #uuid "fa4d60ee-75e2-54e2-b7eb-e1946cf89388" :step/math-academy-id 8722 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139558}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139558 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138558 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138282 :question/id #uuid "5b1ee9ef-da39-5f7a-abb5-98149a765b1f" :question/math-academy-id "e-2565"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3108f0ab-17b1-5241-93d1-97658cf22601" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048725} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050563}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138768} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496315}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between Three Collinear Points"} :step/id #uuid "ab8e5676-8695-5fd6-8d78-6bae4eba68f9" :step/math-academy-id 8723 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139559}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139559 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138559 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138283 :question/id #uuid "cabd7daa-4f22-56ca-8b26-0791857e8eae" :question/math-academy-id "e-2566"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7679cf4-427a-59ed-8b0f-d483af20ede8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138831} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496283}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between More Than Three Collinear Points"} :step/id #uuid "df7668d0-a581-5a0f-a689-b2079711a0d2" :step/math-academy-id 8724 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139560}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139560 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138560 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138284 :question/id #uuid "6df54266-d6d6-54f0-8b61-4b5eabf0fcbc" :question/math-academy-id "e-2567"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c48d15-4749-5c47-bc97-a5584216cf2e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048673} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050452}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138830} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138833} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496245} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558484} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558499}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Value of an Unknown Quantity Using Collinearity"} :step/id #uuid "24ee0c7c-ff8c-5b75-b742-a7f922b432ab" :step/math-academy-id 8725 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139561}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139561 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138561 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138285 :question/id #uuid "fc35d08c-518a-5f03-8e5f-627d58c572d3" :question/math-academy-id "e-2568"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "da4a8391-42b6-510c-8cb4-acbbbe7d1585" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050476}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138767} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138829} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496328}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Value of an Unknown Quantity Using Collinearity and Midpoints"} :step/id #uuid "e6dafd2a-98a4-508e-8294-b9f6ffd16d06" :step/math-academy-id 8726}] :activity/title "The Segment Addition Postulate" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186138602}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558658} :activity/id #uuid "63a069e5-dfcc-5dfc-9bdf-efc2c40a433e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050505 :topic/id #uuid "4ed6acfb-fbd7-5e57-abcc-691c74600c49" :topic/math-academy-id 2278} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558582 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558484 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62234d9b-3ff8-5dee-be89-1c1d5b6dd819" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558054} {:answer/id #uuid "866e0b05-b024-55d0-8a3f-62466373e6a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558128} {:answer/id #uuid "889f2966-4db1-50d6-bdba-0e3b448a1612" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558136} {:answer/id #uuid "bf24cb5e-e54e-5a7c-9d8c-7ebfad3e054d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558248} {:answer/id #uuid "c50bf8df-0f2b-5c83-8628-bfa7b5ba4e93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558262}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558128} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7d6781d-d547-56fd-9e7f-2384c91a2d7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558445}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "09df5c68-2443-52f1-a65f-9fa7f277dc7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-114234" :question/problem "![](images/f2/f22a6d45ad99fa601f45df6d32451c9d004726299130aeb3c6d85e341b275f42.png)\n\nGiven the diagram shown above, what is the value of $x?$" :question/worked-solution "By the segment addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\nAD=AB+BC+CD.\n$$\n\nTo solve for $x,$ we can substitute the given information\n\n$$\nAD=11,\\,AB=2x+3,\\,BC=1-6x,\\,CD=3,\n$$\n\n  \n\nand then solve the resulting equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AD & =AB+BC+CD \\\\ 11 & =(2x+3)+(1-6x)+3 \\\\ 11 & =-4x+7 \\\\ 4x & =-4 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "09de0a38-4cd9-52e8-a3ce-8c1afd886d96"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558652 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558499 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0adfc78d-b274-52a3-b385-1cc24a0e4547" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557892} {:answer/id #uuid "8303b2c9-e028-58ac-b030-adb4dbf505fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558120} {:answer/id #uuid "dc9623aa-1f66-558c-9dc8-3b3345104e29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558302} {:answer/id #uuid "f6e8fb0f-ad49-51be-9c6f-ddad14f234ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558352} {:answer/id #uuid "f89fffb0-f09e-5a27-9b9d-0f4f4f9a72c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558355}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558120} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd21be09-0a94-5fb2-b801-3e3317f3a0d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558450}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "26a43956-daac-5057-b186-4e81cc7a6a8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-72048" :question/problem "![](images/fb/fb879cc65f75feb37766c28176f4ca8e364dd58410b74e625a75ca3e56b6722f.png)\n\nGiven the diagram shown above, what is the value of $x?$" :question/worked-solution "By the segment addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\nAD=AB+BC+CD.\n$$\n\nTo solve for $x,$ we can substitute the given information\n\n$$\nAD=49,\\,AB=2x+1,\\,BC=3x,\\,CD=8,\n$$\n\n  \n\nand then solve the resulting equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AD & =AB+BC+CD \\\\ 49 & =(2x+1)+3x+8 \\\\ 49 & =5x+9 \\\\ 5x & =40 \\\\ x & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8288e638-9825-598a-969c-c812c47b8fa4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558582}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558658 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138832 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9226669b-f95b-5d81-8fa0-34f00060890a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186135941} {:answer/id #uuid "e30cba5b-6769-5bf3-af0c-8e37edf17d82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186135942} {:answer/id #uuid "fc081c99-65c2-58e2-a61f-cc6068725780" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186135943} {:answer/id #uuid "265433d2-3a5b-564b-9daa-e5f30cacd886" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186135944} {:answer/id #uuid "29276cf1-64a6-5a96-bc7a-b06f8006ba08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186135945}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135944} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c78ff5b-52aa-54b9-b865-21f01b6f4d6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186135940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9ea518d5-8976-5fea-b9cf-445893e46637" :question/math-academy-id "q-52749" :question/problem "![](images/d7/d73138fe191ed536299fa6dca679e5948b389d26b4d9e32518eee79123f81f0e.png)\n\nGiven the diagram shown above, what is the measure of $\\overset{―}{BC}?$" :question/worked-solution "The segments addition postulate can be applied to a set of more than three collinear points. In the given situation, it states that\n\n$$\nAD=AB+BC+CD.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values into the equation above, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AD & =AB+BC+CD \\\\ 28 & =9+BC+7 \\\\ 28 & =16+BC \\\\ 28-16 & =BC \\\\ BC & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9406171b-4e2f-594c-98d9-59cfa7fc7631" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558652}}] :activity/title "The Segment Addition Postulate" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557863}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116230} :activity/id #uuid "b0012b09-9794-5364-a20b-bf1c5e744a22" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050828 :topic/id #uuid "69d7c525-79e4-514e-80ba-739018d9e137" :topic/math-academy-id 1587} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116226 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115310 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115139 :question/id #uuid "977cdc31-054e-5910-a649-e604dc4ce31f" :question/math-academy-id "e-8052"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b936abcf-8e73-524c-a9bf-e59b55a48f5f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048187}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495083}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Equations With Even Exponents"} :step/id #uuid "3cf6da35-b32f-503b-b8ea-94efb7441f1c" :step/math-academy-id 30068 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116227}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116227 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115311 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115140 :question/id #uuid "3f41b040-6584-57cd-8d47-b01e071b0fa9" :question/math-academy-id "e-8064"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "23e661ed-3a7b-598a-a7b1-fd5011fe4fb8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115981} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495103} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495166} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558496}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Equations Containing an Expression Raised to an Even Power"} :step/id #uuid "c66f2f53-2687-55ab-8bb5-e1056e5074bc" :step/math-academy-id 30120} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116230 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116534 :tutorial/content "How do we solve an equation with a variable raised to an even exponent, such as\n\n$$\nx^6 = 64?\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation by taking the $6$th root of each side and solving for $x.$ \n\nSince the exponent $(6)$ is even, there will be two real solutions (positive and negative):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{6} & =64 \\\\ \\sqrt[6]{{x}^{6}} & =\\pm \\sqrt[6]{64} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt[6]{{2}^{6}} \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\pm 2.$\n\nIn general, we have the following rule of thumb:\n\n*When $n$ is even, and we take the $n$th root of both sides of $x^n = a$ with $a \\geq 0,$ we get two solutions (positive and negative):* \n\n$$\nx = \\pm\\sqrt[n]{a}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** If $a<0$ and $n$ is even, equation  $x^n = a$ has no solution since, in this case, $x^n \\geq 0$ for any real $x.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6a9834f3-f04b-5340-b297-793072b594d9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4833 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "036c0d84-9bf6-5da8-986c-2ace277257a2" :step/math-academy-id 30128 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116226}}] :activity/title "Solving Equations With Even Exponents Using the Nth Root Method" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186115421}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176820} :activity/id #uuid "4cf8b9a9-4050-5d59-b451-2f66b157c8dd" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050943 :topic/id #uuid "73c67a70-178b-5e99-8871-46ff25f570ba" :topic/math-academy-id 3593} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176820 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177044 :tutorial/content "**Speed** is a special unit rate applied to moving objects. It is defined as *the distance traveled per unit of time*.\n\nWe can calculate the speed of a moving object using the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\text{speed} = \\dfrac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{time}}\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose that a car travels $80$ miles in $2$ hours at a constant rate. What is the speed of the car? \n\nTo find the speed (in miles per hour), we divide the number of miles, $80,$ by the number of hours, $2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{80}{2} = 40.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the car is $40$ miles per hour." :tutorial/id #uuid "e151f782-1f59-5438-92a6-e678c9041d99" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5959 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "136b294f-0279-574d-9755-cfc8cb54f15d" :step/math-academy-id 36386 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176821}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176821 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176030 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175885 :question/id #uuid "696213fa-757d-5450-906a-ffb0638599e8" :question/math-academy-id "e-9704"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88babe91-d8bf-53ba-8d72-35ad35f9253e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050051} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050273}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176328} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268977} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503726}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Speed of an Object"} :step/id #uuid "aed82a50-e10d-539e-895f-797eb5220670" :step/math-academy-id 36387 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176824}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176822 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176031 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175886 :question/id #uuid "1c7eac8b-b163-532f-84f0-10ffd23a67f6" :question/math-academy-id "e-9705"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "534de76f-d4bc-55b5-858e-2b594f63b7bc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050285}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176330} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268995} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500339}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Distance Given Speed and Time"} :step/id #uuid "2e52190a-967b-5eee-8781-4b1c6cdc1bf7" :step/math-academy-id 36388 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176825}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176823 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176032 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175887 :question/id #uuid "cb7d1f42-a5bc-5fba-834c-07a2ceb0644a" :question/math-academy-id "e-9706"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6570253a-b05a-5721-9108-5fddab2b5388" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500355}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Time Given Distance and Speed"} :step/id #uuid "cb434a73-533d-5a74-927b-b8b1bf69153e" :step/math-academy-id 36389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176824 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177045 :tutorial/content "Let's return to the example of a car traveling at ${\\color{red}{40}}$ miles per hour. \n\nIf the car travels at this speed, it covers  a distance of ${\\color{red}{40}}$ miles *every hour*. Therefore, we can deduce the following:\n\n- after ${\\color{blue}{1}}$ hour, the car covers a distance of ${\\color{red}{40}}\\times{\\color{blue}{1}} = 40$ miles\n\n- after ${\\color{blue}{2}}$ hours, the car covers a distance of ${\\color{red}{40}}\\times{\\color{blue}{2}} = 80$ miles\n\n- after ${\\color{blue}{3}}$ hours, the car covers a distance of ${\\color{red}{40}}\\times{\\color{blue}{3}} = 120$ miles\n\n- after ${\\color{blue}{4}}$ hours, the car covers a distance of ${\\color{red}{40}}\\times{\\color{blue}{4}} = 160$ miles\n\n- $\\cdots$\n\nThis suggests the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{distance} = \\text{speed} \\times \\text{time}\n$$\n\nWe can use this formula to calculate the distance traveled by an object moving at a constant speed.\n\nFor instance, suppose an elevator traveled for $1.5$ minutes at a constant speed of $120$ feet per minute. What distance is covered by the elevator?\n\nThe speed is $120$ feet per minute. To find the distance covered in $1.5$ minutes, we multiply the speed by $1.5.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{distance} & =\\text{speed}\\times \\text{time} \\\\  & =120\\times 1.5 \\\\  & =180.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elevator covered a distance of $180$ feet." :tutorial/id #uuid "95f37ad7-7647-57e9-ae85-7a3dcab40f25" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5960 :tutorial/title "Calculating the Distance Traveled by an Object"} :step/id #uuid "02f9ad99-d474-59a2-b870-d4cc8af16598" :step/math-academy-id 36390 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176822}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176825 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177046 :tutorial/content "Let's recall the formula relating distance to speed and time:\n\n$$\n\\text{speed} \\times \\text{time} = \\text{distance}\n$$\n\nUsing the relationship between multiplication and division, we can write this equation as\n\n$$\n\\text{time} = \\text{distance} \\div \\text{speed}\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{time} = \\dfrac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{speed}}.\n$$\n\nWe can use this formula to calculate the time taken for an object moving at a particular speed to cover a certain distance. \n\nFor example, suppose a plane flew for $3\\,120$ kilometers at a constant speed of $520$ kilometers per hour. For how long was the plane in motion?\n\nTo determine the time the plane was in motion, we divide the distance, $3\\,120$ kilometers, by the speed, $520$ kilometers per hour. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{time} & =\\frac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{speed}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\,120}{520} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\,120÷10}{520÷10} \\\\  & =\\frac{312}{52} \\\\  & =312÷52 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the plane was in motion for $6$ hours." :tutorial/id #uuid "76064dfb-b2e4-56a5-95f7-2a97b1fb392b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5961 :tutorial/title "Calculating the Total Time an Object is in Motion"} :step/id #uuid "1aec8595-c434-5eab-92bb-a1d9a3d99a15" :step/math-academy-id 36391 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176823}}] :activity/title "Speed as a Unit Rate" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176136}]}
